.TH VV 1 "August 2026" "vv 1.18.6" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vv \- universal genomic file viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vv
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIFILE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBvv\fR is a fast command-line viewer for tabular and bioinformatics file
formats. It prints the first \fIN\fR rows as an ASCII table (similar to
\fBpandas.DataFrame.head()\fR), followed by a schema summary and file
metadata. When stdout and stdin are both terminals, an interactive
ncurses row browser is opened automatically.
.PP
Supported formats:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.parquet\fR \(en Apache Parquet
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.arrow\fR, \fB.feather\fR \(en Arrow IPC and Feather v2
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.lociss\fR \(en LociSSD sorted-interval; either v3 Parquet (auto-detected via
the \fBlociSSD_manifest\fR key in the Parquet footer; the derived
\fBMaxEndSoFar\fR column is hidden from human-facing views but kept in
\fB\-\-tsv\fR / \fB\-\-csv\fR / \fB\-\-parquet\fR output), or a v4 \(lqcolblock\(rq
custom binary container (magic \fBLSB1\fR + a zone-map index of zstd column
chunks \(en the index is stored inline in a single self-contained file (v4.1,
default) or in a sidecar \fB.idx\fR (legacy v4)). Both are dispatched by magic and
support \fB\-r\fR region queries
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.bam\fR, \fB.cram\fR, \fB.sam\fR \(en sequence alignments (htslib)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.vcf\fR, \fB.vcf.gz\fR \(en variant calls
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.gff\fR, \fB.gff3\fR, \fB.gtf\fR (and \fB.gz\fR variants) \(en genome
annotations
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.bed\fR, \fB.bed.gz\fR \(en genomic intervals (auto-named
\fIBED3..BED12\fR by column type signature)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.narrowPeak\fR / \fB.broadPeak\fR / \fB.gappedPeak\fR /
\fB.bedGraph\fR (\fB.bg\fR) / \fB.tagAlign\fR (plus \fB.gz\fR) \(en
ENCODE peak and signal formats. Read as BED variants with
known-typed extra columns: \fIsignalValue\fR, \fIpValue\fR,
\fIqValue\fR, \fIpeak\fR, \fIvalue\fR, \fIsequence\fR. Both
non-gzip and gzip-compressed variants supported
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.bb\fR, \fB.bigBed\fR \(en UCSC bigBed (via vendored libBigWig); the
embedded autoSql is parsed so the trailing fields (\fBname\fR,
\fBscore\fR, \fBsignalValue\fR, \fBpValue\fR, …) become typed Arrow
columns
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.bw\fR, \fB.bigWig\fR \(en UCSC bigWig; rendered as four columns
(\fBchrom\fR, \fBstart\fR, \fBend\fR, \fBvalue\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.sqlite\fR, \fB.sqlite3\fR, \fB.db\fR \(en SQLite databases. Each
user table becomes a TUI tab (\fBTab\fR / \fBShift+Tab\fR cycle).
Column types follow SQLite type affinity: TEXT \(-> string, INTEGER
\(-> int64, REAL \(-> double, BLOB \(-> binary. Read-only access.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.xlsx\fR, \fB.xlsm\fR \(en Excel workbooks (Office Open XML). Each
sheet becomes a TUI tab. Cell text is streamed through Arrow's CSV
reader for column-type inference (int / float / bool / ISO 8601 date /
string). The legacy binary \fB.xls\fR format is not supported. Requires
\fBlibxlsxio\fR (\fBxlsxio\fR AUR on Arch, \fBlibxlsxio-dev\fR on
Debian/Ubuntu, \fBbrew install xlsxio\fR on macOS).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.h5ad\fR, \fB.h5\fR, \fB.hdf5\fR, \fB.loom\fR \(en HDF5 containers
read via libhdf5. AnnData (\fB.h5ad\fR) is detected by the root
\fBencoding-type\fR attribute (or modern heuristic /obs + /var + X);
the layout decodes into a summary tab plus \fBobs\fR, \fBvar\fR,
\fBX\fR (densified first-N-row preview for CSR sparse), and one tab
per entry under \fBobsm\fR / \fBvarm\fR / \fBlayers\fR. Categoricals
(\fBcodes\fR / \fBcategories\fR groups) decode back to string columns.
Generic HDF5 files surface a \fBhierarchy\fR tab listing every
group / dataset with path / kind / shape / dtype, plus one tab per
1-D or 2-D dataset (capped at 32 datasets / 32 columns). \fBuns\fR
(unstructured) decoding and CSC sparse preview are deferred.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.ods\fR \(en OpenDocument Spreadsheet. Each sheet becomes a TUI
tab. Hand-rolled parser: minizip inflates \fBcontent.xml\fR from the
\&.ods ZIP, expat SAX-parses the SpreadsheetML payload. Cells with a
typed \fBoffice:value\fR (numeric / date / boolean) use that
canonical value; string cells use the rendered text. Empty runs
collapsed via \fBtable:number-columns-repeated\fR are honoured.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.pileup\fR, \fB.mpileup\fR, \fB.pile\fR (plus \fB.gz\fR) \(en
samtools mpileup output. Per-base pileup: \fBchrom\fR, \fBpos\fR
(1-based), \fBref\fR, then a \fBdepth\fR / \fBbases\fR / \fBquals\fR
triplet per sample. vv counts tabs to infer the sample count;
multi-sample triplets are suffixed (\fBdepth_1\fR, …). Range
queries work on bgzipped + tabix-indexed files (\fBtabix -s 1 -b 2
-e 2\fR). Pass \fB--decode-pileup\fR to replace the packed bases /
qualities with typed per-allele counts: \fBA\fR, \fBC\fR, \fBG\fR,
\fBT\fR, \fBN\fR, \fBdel_placeholder\fR, \fBins\fR, \fBdel\fR,
\fBfwd\fR, \fBrev\fR, \fBmean_qual\fR (Phred mean). Multi-sample
files get the same set suffixed per sample. Pass \fB--pileup\fR on
a BAM/CRAM to walk the alignments through htslib's
\fBbam_plp_auto\fR engine and emit the same mpileup rows directly,
without a separate \fBsamtools mpileup\fR step. Range queries
require an index (\fB.bai\fR / \fB.csi\fR / \fB.crai\fR). Pass
\fB\-f\fR / \fB\-\-fasta\fR \fIref.fa\fR (which needs a \fB.fai\fR
index) to fill the ref column from the reference and render bases
matching it as \fB.\fR (forward) / \fB,\fR (reverse), byte-for-byte
like \fBsamtools mpileup \-f\fR (vv applies no BAQ, i.e. \fB\-B\fR);
without it ref is \fBN\fR and bases render as their literal letter
case-by-strand.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.orc\fR \(en Apache ORC (Optimized Row Columnar). Columnar storage
used by Hadoop / Hive / Spark. One ORC stripe is treated as one chunk
for lazy loading. Read via Arrow's ORC adapter \(em Arrow must be
built with \fB-DARROW_ORC=ON\fR, which the Apache Arrow apt repo,
Homebrew \fBapache-arrow\fR, and the conda-forge \fBpyarrow\fR wheel
all have by default. The AlmaLinux 8 static binary currently does
\fBnot\fR include ORC; \fBvv\fR reports "compiled without ORC support"
when invoked on an .orc file there.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.txt\fR, \fB.text\fR, \fB.log\fR (plus \fB.gz\fR) \(en plain text.
Also the fallback for any file no other format claims: an unknown
extension whose content sniffs as text is shown as text, with a note
on stderr saying so. Viewed in the interactive TUI like \fBless -SN\fR
\(em a line-number gutter, long lines chopped at the screen edge with
\fBh\fR / \fBl\fR scrolling sideways and \fB0\fR returning to column 0,
\fB/\fR search and \fB&\fR filter over the \fBline\fR column, and one tab
per file. Written back verbatim when stdout is not a terminal, so
\fBvv f.log > copy\fR round-trips byte for byte; on screen, SGR colour
is honoured but every other escape sequence is dropped whole.
Binary input is refused rather than dumped \(em \fBvv\fR has no hex
view. See \fB--text\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.md\fR, \fB.markdown\fR, \fB.mdown\fR, \fB.mkd\fR \(en CommonMark
+ GFM markdown rendered as ANSI to stdout. Headings, bold / italic /
strike / code, lists, block quotes, fenced code blocks, horizontal
rules, and links are styled per the active \fB--theme\fR. GFM
\fB|\fR-tables are extracted and rendered through vv's normal table
view (column types inferred from cell contents \(em a benchmark
column of integers stays an int64). Local PNG / JPEG / GIF images
display inline on kitty, iTerm2, and WezTerm terminals via their
graphics protocols; everything else (remote URLs, SVG, terminals
without graphics support) falls back to a \fB[image: alt-text]\fR
stub. Pipe to \fBless -R\fR to scroll.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.2bit\fR \(en UCSC 2bit sequence container; rendered as a per-
sequence index (\fBname\fR, \fBlength\fR, \fBn_blocks\fR,
\fBmask_blocks\fR). DNA bases themselves are not decoded — too large
for chromosome-scale references; use \fBtwoBitToFa\fR for that.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.fa\fR, \fB.fasta\fR, \fB.fna\fR, \fB.faa\fR, \fB.ffn\fR,
\fB.frn\fR (and \fB.gz\fR) \(en sequences (FASTA)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.fq\fR, \fB.fastq\fR (and \fB.gz\fR) \(en sequencing reads (FASTQ)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.tsv\fR, \fB.csv\fR (and \fB.gz\fR) \(en delimited text
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.bcf\fR \(en binary VCF (htslib)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.paf\fR, \fB.paf.gz\fR \(en minimap2 pairwise alignments
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.npy\fR \(en a single NumPy array; \fB.npz\fR \(en a NumPy archive,
one array per TUI tab. 1-D and 2-D arrays become rows and columns; 3-D+
arrays are sliced along the leading axis, navigable with \fB[\fR / \fB]\fR
in the viewer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB-\fR \(en read text format from stdin (auto-detects gzip)
.PP
Unknown extensions are sniffed by magic bytes and delimiter heuristics.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Display
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-interactive
Force the ncurses row browser even when stdout is not a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-interactive
Force plain ASCII table output even on a terminal.
.TP
.BI \-n " ROWS"
Number of rows to display (default: 10, 0 = all).
.TP
.BI \-\-tail " N"
Show the last \fIN\fR rows instead of the first \fIN\fR. Reads the
whole source (filtered by \fB\-\-filter\fR if any) and slices the
tail. Combines with every view / export mode. For streaming
sources (BAM, BCF, FASTX, …) this implies a full scan.
.TP
.BI \-w " WIDTH"
Maximum cell width in the ASCII table (default: 32).
.TP
.BI \-c " COLS"
Maximum number of columns to display (default: all).
.TP
.BI \-\-select " terms"
Project a subset of columns. \fIterms\fR is a comma-separated list;
the output follows the order given, so \fB\-\-select End,Start\fR also
reorders. Each term is one of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
an exact column name \(en \fBChromosome\fR. An exact name always wins over
pattern interpretation, so a column literally called \fB2\-4\fR or
\fBlog2\-ratio\fR stays addressable;
.IP \(bu 2
a glob \(en \fBchr*\fR, \fB?_pct\fR (\fBfnmatch\fR(3); \fB*\fR and \fB?\fR,
case-sensitive). Quote it, or the shell expands it first;
.IP \(bu 2
a 1-based inclusive index range \(en \fB2\-4\fR, or \fB5\-\fR to the last
column;
.IP \(bu 2
a type class \(en \fB@numeric\fR, \fB@string\fR, \fB@list\fR, \fB@bool\fR,
\fB@temporal\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
an exclusion \(en \fB!TERM\fR removes everything \fITERM\fR matches. A
leading exclusion starts from the full set for the current mode.
.RE
.IP
Duplicates collapse to their first occurrence. A name that does not exist,
and a pattern that matches nothing, are both errors (reported differently) \(en
neither is silently ignored, since an empty match would quietly drop data
from an export. In display modes the hidden-by-default columns (e.g.
LociSSD's \fBMaxEndSoFar\fR) stay hidden; in export modes
(\fB\-\-tsv\fR / \fB\-\-csv\fR / \fB\-\-json\fR / \fB\-\-parquet\fR)
the explicit list is honoured exactly.
.TP
.BI \-\-expand " col"
Unpack a packed \fBkey=value\fR column into real columns, appended to the
schema. Written for VCF \fBINFO\fR and GFF/GTF \fBattributes\fR, which carry
the payload of those formats as a single opaque string \(en once expanded the
keys behave like any other column, so
.B \-\-select ,
.B \-\-filter ,
.B \-\-unique
and the Parquet / Arrow writers all see them.
.IP
For VCF the key set and their types come from the \fB##INFO=<...>\fR
declarations, which needs no data scan. A key declared
\fBNumber=A\fR/\fBR\fR/\fBG\fR/\fB.\fR (\fBAD\fR, \fBPL\fR, …) holds one
value per allele or genotype, so it stays text rather than being typed numeric
and coming back null.
.IP
GFF/GTF declares nothing, so the keys are taken from the first chunk in
first-seen order, capped at 256. A repeated key (gencode repeats \fBtag\fR)
yields one column, from its first occurrence. Consequently a
.B \-n
preview and a full scan can legitimately report different column sets for the
same GTF.
.IP
The original column is kept and existing column indices do not move, so a
projection or region-column auto-detection that worked before is unaffected. A
column that does not look like a key=value list is refused rather than
expanded into one column per distinct value.
.TP
.BI \-\-filter " expr"
Keep only rows matching \fIexpr\fR. Grammar:
\fB<column> <op> <value>\fR joined by \fBAND\fR / \fBOR\fR. Operators:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB==\fR, \fB!=\fR, \fB<\fR, \fB<=\fR, \fB>\fR, \fB>=\fR \(en compare;
.IP \(bu 2
\fB~\fR, \fB!~\fR \(en ECMAScript regular expression, unanchored (use
\fB^\fR / \fB$\fR to anchor). A malformed pattern is a parse error, not a
silently-empty result;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcontains\fR, \fBstartswith\fR, \fBendswith\fR \(en substring tests;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBin (\fIa\fB, \fIb\fB, \fIc\fB)\fR and \fBnot in (\...)\fR \(en set
membership. On a numeric column the members are compared numerically;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBis null\fR and \fBis not null\fR \(en match the column's actual nulls,
the rows \fB\-\-describe\fR counts but nothing could previously select.
.RE
.IP
The word operators are operators only in operator position, so a column
genuinely named \fBin\fR, \fBis\fR or \fBcontains\fR stays filterable.
Values: integer, float, single- or double-quoted string. Example:
\fB\-\-filter 'Chromosome == "chr1" AND Score > 0.5'\fR. Evaluated
per row after the source's own pruning (tabix region, LociSSD
row-group stats) so combining with \fB\-r\fR is fine.
.TP
.B \-\-schema
Print column names, Arrow types, nullability, and the file-info
footer (row groups, compression, creator) and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-tab " NAME"
View a named component tab instead of the first one: AnnData
\fBobs\fR / \fBvar\fR / \fBX\fR, a workbook sheet, and so on. Lets the
CLI reach the data components that are otherwise only navigable in the
interactive TUI, e.g. \fBvv cells.h5ad \-\-tab obs \-n 20\fR. Matching is
case-insensitive; an unknown name lists the available tabs. Large
components are shown as a capped preview (the footer notes the full size).
.TP
.B \-\-describe
Per-column statistics: count, nulls, min, max, mean (numeric),
distinct count (string, capped at 16). Respects \fB\-\-select\fR
and \fB\-\-filter\fR. Currently scans the data; doesn't yet read
Parquet's per-row-group statistics. Add \fB\-\-json\fR (array) or
\fB\-\-ndjson\fR (one object per line) for machine-readable stats
(integers exact, floats round-trippable, strings JSON-escaped;
numeric columns omit \fBdistinct\fR, all-null columns report
\fBnull\fR min/max/mean).
.TP
.B \-\-count
Print the number of rows and exit. Reflects a \fB\-r\fR region
(taken from the source's row count, so it is instant on formats
that carry a count in metadata) and, when \fB\-\-filter\fR is
given, counts only the matching rows (a scan).
.TP
.B \-\-stats
Print Parquet metadata (row groups, compressed / uncompressed sizes,
codecs, per-column sizes and null counts) without decoding any data.
For non-Parquet sources, falls back to \fB\-\-schema\fR. Useful for
"what's in this 50 GB file from a colleague?".
.TP
.BI \-\-unique " cols"
Distinct-value counts per column, comma-separated. Each block lists up
to 50 most frequent values; further values are summarised. Respects
\fB\-\-filter\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-sample " N"
Uniformly random N rows (reservoir sampling) instead of head-N. The
sample is the row set; combine with \fB\-\-tsv\fR, \fB\-\-parquet\fR,
or any view mode for output. Honours \fB\-\-filter\fR (filter first,
then sample). Reads the whole file; document upstream if you only want
sample-from-the-first-M-rows.
.TP
.B \-\-text
Read the file as plain text whatever its extension says. The escape
hatch for a textual-but-tabular file: \fBvv --text notes.md\fR shows
the markdown source instead of rendering it, \fBvv --text data.csv\fR
shows the raw lines instead of a table. The content is still sniffed,
so \fBvv --text foo.bam\fR is refused rather than dumped.
.TP
.B \-\-validate
Check LociSSD invariants: required columns present with correct
types; the manifest covers every row exactly once; rows are sorted
by \fB(Start, End)\fR within each chromosome; \fBMaxEndSoFar[i]\fR
equals the running \fBmax(End)\fR within each chromosome; and the
\fBChromosome\fR label at each row matches the manifest. Prints
\fBPASS\fR / \fBFAIL\fR per check and exits non-zero on any failure
\(en suitable as a CI gate after a writer change.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-index
Suppress the row-index column.
.TP
.BI \-\-color [= WHEN ]
\fIWHEN\fR is \fBauto\fR (default), \fBalways\fR, or \fBnever\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " NAME"
Pick a color palette. Built-in themes:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdefault\fR \(en the original vv palette: bright accents on the
terminal's default background.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdark\fR \(en slightly punchier accents, assumes a near-black
background.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlight\fR \(en darker mid-saturation accents for terminals with a
light background; disables the zebra-stripe background tint so the
table reads cleanly on white.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsolarized-dark\fR / \fBsolarized-light\fR \(en Ethan Schoonover's
Solarized palette. \fBsolarized\fR is accepted as a synonym for
\fBsolarized-dark\fR.
.RE
.IP
Affects both the non-interactive ASCII table (via ANSI escapes) and
the ncurses TUI. On terminals with fewer than 256 colors, each theme
falls back to a 16-color twin.
.IP
Inside the TUI, press \fBT\fR to open the theme-picker overlay; the
choice is saved to the user config file and re-applied on the next
launch. See \fBFILES\fR below.
.TP
.B \-\-vertical
\fIVertical head\fR. Transpose the preview so each input field becomes a row
and each record becomes a column; show as many records as fit in the
terminal width. Implies \fB\-\-no\-interactive\fR. Useful for wide tables
that would otherwise need horizontal scrolling. Auto-enabled when the
binary is invoked as \fBvh\fR (a symlink to \fBvv\fR).
.SS Parquet output
.TP
.BI \-\-parquet " FILE"
Stream the input into a Parquet file at \fIFILE\fR. Combines with
\fB\-c\fR (column subset) and \fB\-n\fR (row cap) to convert any supported
input \(en BED, TSV, CSV, VCF, BCF, FASTA, FASTQ, BAM \(en into a Parquet
file. Stays streaming for forward-only sources (BAM/FASTQ).
\fIFILE\fR may be \fB-\fR to write to stdout; the data is spooled to a
temp file first because Parquet's footer-at-end needs seekable writes.
.TP
.BI \-\-arrow " FILE" \fR, \fB\-\-feather\fR " FILE"
Stream the input into an Arrow IPC file (a.k.a. Feather v2) at
\fIFILE\fR \(en the zero-copy interchange format for pandas / polars /
R \fBarrow\fR. Same column / row / filter handling as \fB\-\-parquet\fR;
\fB\-\-feather\fR is an alias. \fIFILE\fR may be \fB-\fR for stdout.
.TP
.BI \-\-compression " CODEC"
Codec for \fB\-\-parquet\fR output: \fBzstd\fR (default), \fBsnappy\fR,
\fBgzip\fR, \fBlz4\fR, \fBnone\fR. For \fB\-\-arrow\fR / \fB\-\-feather\fR,
only \fBzstd\fR (default), \fBlz4\fR, or \fBnone\fR (Arrow IPC body
compression).
.SS Delimited output
.TP
.B \-\-tsv
Stream tab-separated values to stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-csv
Stream comma-separated values to stdout.
.TP
.BI \-\-delimiter " SEP"
Custom single-character field separator.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write a JSON array of row objects to stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-ndjson
Write one JSON object per line (JSON Lines / NDJSON). Pipe-friendly
for \fBjq(1)\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-md ", " \-\-markdown
Write a GitHub-flavored markdown table to stdout. Pipes inside cells
are escaped as \fB\\|\fR and newlines become \fB<br>\fR. Honours
\fB\-\-select\fR, \fB\-\-filter\fR, \fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-no\-header\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-header
Omit the header row in delimited output.
.SS Visualization
.TP
.B \-\-heatmap
Render the numeric columns as a colour heatmap in the terminal: a
\fIrows \(mu numeric-columns\fR image, globally normalised (a viridis
palette). Non-finite cells (NaN/Inf) and non-numeric columns are skipped.
When stdout is not a terminal (redirected to a file or pipe), a plain
ASCII intensity grid is written instead of raw terminal escape sequences.
.TP
.BI \-\-image\-mode " HOW"
Heatmap rendering backend: \fBauto\fR (default \(en kitty graphics if the
terminal supports it, else half-block), \fBkitty\fR, \fBsixel\fR,
\fBhalfblock\fR, or \fBascii\fR (plain text).
.SS Range queries
.TP
.BI \-\-region\-cols " NAMES"
Three comma-separated column names (chrom,start,end) for region
queries on plain Parquet when auto-detection picks the wrong fields.
Common names \fIChromosome\fR / \fIChrom\fR / \fIChr\fR /
\fIPOS\fR / \fIchromStart\fR / \fIchromEnd\fR are tried automatically.
.TP
.BI \-\-coords " KIND"
Coordinate convention for the \fB\-r\fR argument. Two values:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUCSC\fR (default) \(en 0-based, half-open intervals. This is the
convention introduced by Jim Kent's UCSC Genome Browser source tree in
2000 and used today by BED, bigBed, bigWig, BAM, and the LociSSD
Parquet format. Aliases: \fBKent\fR, \fB0-based\fR, \fBbed\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNCBI\fR \(en 1-based, fully-closed intervals. This is the convention
inherited from GenBank (1982) and EMBL-Bank (1980); it is what VCF, GFF,
tabix region strings, and the samtools/bcftools command line all use.
Aliases: \fB1-based\fR, \fBGenBank\fR, \fBtabix\fR.
.RE
.IP
\fB\-\-regions\-file\fR entries are always parsed as BED (UCSC
0-based) per the BED spec, regardless of \fB\-\-coords\fR.
.TP
.BI \-r ", " \-\-region " REGION"
Filter to records overlapping \fIREGION\fR (e.g. \fBchr1:1000-2000\fR).
Multiple regions may be given comma-separated. Open-ended forms
(\fBchr1:\fR \(en whole chromosome, \fBchr1:78-\fR, \fBchr1:-99\fR) are
accepted. Coordinates follow the UCSC convention (0-based, half-open)
by default; pass \fB\-\-coords NCBI\fR for 1-based inclusive
(samtools / tabix / VCF style).
.IP
If the queried chromosome isn't in the file but its UCSC\(->Ensembl
counterpart is, vv retries with the alias and notes the swap on stderr
\(en so \fB\-r chr1\fR works on a file that names the contig \fB1\fR, and
vice versa. This is limited to \fBhuman and mouse\fR standard chromosomes
(autosomes \fB1\fR\(en\fB22\fR, \fBX\fR, \fBY\fR, and the mitochondrion
\fBchrM\fR \(<> \fBMT\fR); scaffolds, patches and alt-contigs are never
remapped.
.IP
Supported on:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
indexed \fB.bam\fR / \fB.cram\fR (needs \fB.bai\fR, \fB.csi\fR or
\fB.crai\fR from \fBsamtools index\fR); a plain \fB.sam\fR has no
index and is rejected;
.IP \(bu 2
tabix-indexed \fB.vcf.gz\fR, \fB.bed.gz\fR, \fB.gff.gz\fR, and
\fB.tsv.gz\fR (via htslib's tabix iterator);
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.lociss\fR (LociSSD Parquet \(en pruning uses the manifest plus
Parquet row-group statistics on the \fBStart\fR and \fBMaxEndSoFar\fR
columns, then a per-row predicate inside surviving row groups).
.RE
.IP
Plain \fB.parquet\fR with chrom/start/end columns works without a
LociSSD manifest (see \fB\-\-region\-cols\fR for column-name overrides).
.IP
A format with no region index (Arrow IPC, ORC, FASTA, SQLite, spreadsheets,
\&...) prints a warning to standard error and shows the whole file, rather
than silently presenting an unfiltered result as a region query.
.TP
.BI \-\-window " REGION"
Alias of \fB\-r\fR / \fB\-\-region\fR \(en the name LociSSD readers often
reach for first.
.TP
.BI \-\-regions\-file " FILE"
Read additional windows from a BED-formatted file (first three
TSV columns: \fBchrom\fR, \fBstart\fR, \fBend\fR; UCSC convention,
0-based half-open). Lines starting with \fB#\fR / \fBtrack\fR /
\fBbrowser\fR are skipped. Combines with explicit \fB\-r\fR; both
lists are taken.
.TP
.BI \-\-slop " N"
Pad every window by \fIN\fR base pairs on each side (i.e. expand
\fBstart\fR by \-N and \fBend\fR by +N; start is clamped at 0).
Applied after \fB\-\-regions-file\fR. Equivalent to
\fBbedtools slop(1)\fR.
.IP
For BCF, range queries require an index (\fB.csi\fR or \fB.tbi\fR
next to the file). If absent, \fBvv\fR prints a hint pointing to
\fBbcftools index(1)\fR.
.SS Performance
.TP
.BI \-@ ", " \-\-threads " N"
Number of worker threads for I/O and decoding. \fB0\fR (default) auto-
detects (cores/2, capped at 8). Drives Arrow's CPU thread pool, htslib
BAM/CRAM decompression, and BGZF decompression for FASTA/FASTQ.
.TP
.BI \-\-decode\-threads " N"
Override the Arrow CPU thread-pool size used for Parquet column decode
and CSV/TSV parallel parsing. Defaults to \fB\-\-threads\fR; raising it
above can give 1.5–2× on cold reads where decompression is the
bottleneck and the rest of the system has idle cores. Bounded at twice
\fBhardware_concurrency()\fR.
.SS Other
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.SH KEYS (interactive TUI)
.TP
.B Arrow keys / hjkl
Move by row / column.
.TP
.B PgUp, PgDn, g, G
Page up / down, jump to top / bottom.
.TP
.B / and ?
Search forward (\fB/\fR) or backward (\fB?\fR), \fBless(1)\fR-style.
The query is a case-insensitive ECMAScript regular expression; if the
pattern is invalid it is matched literally instead. \fBn\fR continues in
the same direction, \fBN\fR reverses it. All matches in the visible area
are highlighted; the n/N target is rendered with reverse video.
\fBEsc\fR clears.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the detail pane for the highlighted row.
.TP
.B "," / "."
Narrow / widen the column under the cursor.
.TP
.B z
Toggle frozen first column (pin column 0 at the left edge while
scrolling sideways).
.TP
.B S
Per-column stats popup for the column under the cursor: count,
null count, min, max, mean (numeric), distinct-value count (strings,
capped at 16). Triggers a full-file scan with progress shown in the
status bar. Any key dismisses the popup.
.TP
.B s
Sort by the active column. Numeric types sort by raw value, others
lexicographically; nulls go last regardless of direction. Repeated
\fBs\fR on the same column toggles ascending / descending; \fBs\fR
on a different column re-sorts ascending. \fBu\fR clears the sort.
Builds an in-memory row permutation; cost is one full-file scan.
.TP
.B c
Open the show/hide columns picker. \fBj\fR / \fBk\fR move the
cursor, \fBSpace\fR / \fBEnter\fR toggle the column under the
cursor, \fBEsc\fR / \fBq\fR / \fBc\fR closes the overlay. At least
one column stays visible.
.TP
.B &
Live filter: open a \fB&<expression>\fR input bar at the bottom of
the screen. The grammar matches \fB\-\-filter\fR \(en
\fB<col> <op> <literal>\fR joined with \fBAND\fR / \fBOR\fR, ops
\fB==\fR \fB!=\fR \fB<\fR \fB<=\fR \fB>\fR \fB>=\fR. Enter commits;
non-matching rows are hidden from the display. An empty commit
clears the filter, and \fBEsc\fR in normal mode also clears an
active filter before quitting. Composes with \fBs\fR (sort) and
\fB/\fR (search).
.TP
.B mouse wheel
Scroll three rows up / down.
.TP
.B mouse click
On a column header: toggle sort by that column (same as moving
\fBleft_col\fR to it and pressing \fBs\fR). On a data row:
scroll the clicked row to the top of the viewport and make its
column the focus for follow-up \fBS\fR / \fBy\fR / \fBs\fR.
.TP
.B mouse double-click
On a data row: open the detail pane for that row (same as
pressing \fBEnter\fR).
.TP
.B Shift+drag
While vv has mouse capture, the terminal stops handling its own
click-and-drag selection. Hold \fBShift\fR to bypass the capture
and let the terminal emulator do its native text selection (works
in xterm, alacritty, kitty, foot, wezterm, iTerm2, gnome-terminal,
konsole). Alternatively, use \fBy\fR to copy the cell at the top
of the viewport to the system clipboard via OSC52.
.TP
.B Tab, Shift+Tab
Next / previous file tab. With multiple positional arguments on the
command line each file becomes a tab; switching is instant because
the chunk cache, sort, filter, column-hide, and search anchor are
preserved per tab. The status bar shows the active tab number and
basename. Single-file invocations don't show a tab indicator.
.TP
.B :
Vim-style typed-command prompt at the bottom of the screen.
Recognised verbs:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB:<N>\fR \(en jump to row \fIN\fR (matches the row-index column;
out-of-range values clamp to the last page).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB:q\fR / \fB:quit\fR \(en quit.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB:theme NAME\fR \(en switch to a named theme (same names as
\fB\-\-theme\fR / the \fBT\fR overlay); the choice is persisted to
\fI~/.config/vv/config\fR.
.RE
.IP
Errors stay in the input bar so the line can be edited and
re-submitted. \fBEsc\fR cancels.
.TP
.B H, F1
Open / close the help overlay listing every keybinding.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.SH ALIAS / vh
\fBvh\fR is a symlink to \fBvv\fR that defaults to \fB\-\-vertical\fR mode.
\fBvh wide.parquet\fR prints a transposed head where the user scrolls
vertically through fields rather than horizontally through columns.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
First 10 rows of a Parquet file with a nice ASCII table:
.RS
.EX
vv data.parquet
.EE
.RE
.PP
Stream a 100-row TSV preview from a multi-GB Parquet file (uses fast path):
.RS
.EX
vv \-\-tsv \-n 100 huge.parquet
.EE
.RE
.PP
Region preview of a LociSSD Parquet file (no tabix needed):
.RS
.EX
vv \-r chr1:78\-99 peaks.lociss
.EE
.RE
.PP
Region query on a tabix-indexed VCF:
.RS
.EX
vv \-r chr1:1000000-1100000 variants.vcf.gz
.EE
.RE
.PP
Multi-region tabix query on a BED file:
.RS
.EX
vv \-r 'chr1:100-200,chr2:500-1000' regions.bed.gz
.EE
.RE
.PP
Open an Arrow IPC file in the interactive browser:
.RS
.EX
vv data.arrow
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert a BED file to Parquet (zstd, all rows):
.RS
.EX
vv \-\-parquet peaks.parquet peaks.bed
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert a tabix-filtered VCF region to Parquet:
.RS
.EX
vv \-r chr1:1000000\-2000000 \-\-parquet region.parquet variants.vcf.gz
.EE
.RE
.PP
Multi-threaded scan of a 5 GB BAM:
.RS
.EX
vv \-@ 4 \-n 1000 alignments.bam
.EE
.RE
.PP
Vertical head on a wide Parquet file (50+ columns) that doesn't fit:
.RS
.EX
vh wide.parquet
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/vv/config
.RS
User configuration file (default
\fI$HOME/.config/vv/config\fR). Plain INI-style \fBkey = value\fR
syntax; lines starting with \fB#\fR are comments. CLI flags always
win over the config file.
.IP
Recognised keys:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtheme\fR \(en one of \fBdefault\fR / \fBdark\fR / \fBlight\fR /
\fBsolarized-dark\fR / \fBsolarized-light\fR. Written by the
in-TUI theme picker (\fBT\fR key).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbackground\fR \(en \fBdark\fR or \fBlight\fR; what
\fBVV_BACKGROUND\fR sets, permanently. Also skips the OSC 11
terminal background query, which web consoles answer too slowly
to be useful.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax_col_width\fR \(en integer \(>= 4; the \fB\-w\fR default.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthreads\fR \(en integer \(>= 1; the \fB\-@\fR default.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBscrolloff\fR \(en integer \(>= 0; TUI rows kept between the
cursor and the viewport edge (config-only, no flag).
.IP
Unknown keys are ignored, so a config written for a newer vv does
not break an older one. Writes are atomic (\fI.tmp\fR + rename) and
preserve any other lines you've added by hand.
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to any non-empty value, disables coloured output (see
\fIhttps://no-color.org\fR). An explicit \fB\-\-color=always\fR or
\fB\-\-color=never\fR overrides it.
.TP
.B VV_CATEGORY_DICT_CAP
Maximum number of categories an AnnData categorical \fIobs\fR/\fIvar\fR column
may have before it is shown as integer codes rather than decoded labels
(default \fB1000000\fR).
.TP
.B VV_BACKGROUND
Force the terminal background the TUI assumes for its alternating-row (zebra)
stripe: \fBlight\fR or \fBdark\fR. By default vv queries the terminal (OSC 11,
then \fBCOLORFGBG\fR) and assumes dark if that fails. Set \fBlight\fR when the
zebra stripe is a hard dark band on a light-background terminal (e.g.
JupyterLab's web terminal) and auto-detection didn't catch it.
.SH FORMATS
.B vv \-\-formats
prints the authoritative table of supported formats with their capability
columns (gz variants, region queries, component tabs, streaming vs random
access);
.B vv \-\-formats \-\-json
emits the same table as JSON. The shell completions are checked against it in
CI, so the two cannot drift.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime or usage error: the input could not be opened, was an unsupported or
malformed format, a read failed, an unknown option was given, or no input file
was supplied. Also returned when a request could not be honoured in full \(em
an unknown
.B \-\-select
column, an unparseable
.B \-\-filter
expression, a read error that truncated the output, or more than one input
file in a non\-interactive mode (extra positionals are only meaningful in the
interactive viewer, which gives each file a tab).
.TP
.B 2
An invalid command\-line option argument: a flag was missing its required value,
or a value was not one of the accepted choices (e.g.
\fB\-\-image\-mode\fR, \fB\-\-coords\fR).
